*Counts are total Stage-3 verdicts per pass. v1 IN-scope candidates: 88 (70 high + 18 low after one PRISMA exclusion). v2 IN-scope candidates: 179 (120 high + 59 low). Combined Step-4 candidate set after Step-3.5 sub-screen: 179 QC_FOR_IMAGING + 12 Step-3.5 promotes = 191 records.*

# 9. Data items and extraction plan (Stage 4 onwards)

For each paper that survives Stage 4 full-text screening, the following data items will be extracted by an AI-assisted extraction pipeline with mandatory human cross-check by a co-author:

## 9.1 Bibliographic

- DOI, title, authors, year, journal, publisher, country of corresponding author, funding declared.

## 9.2 Quantum-computing characterisation

- Paradigm (QML, VQE, QAOA, quantum kernel, FRQI, NEQR, hybrid quantum-classical, other; free text for novel paradigms).

- Hardware platform (vendor, qubit count, qubit modality: superconducting, trapped-ion, photonic, neutral-atom, annealing). Specify whether real hardware or simulator.

- Simulator framework (Qiskit Aer, PennyLane, Cirq, Microsoft QDK, Strawberry Fields, other).

- Circuit depth, parameter count, ansatz family if applicable.

- Image encoding method (amplitude encoding, basis encoding, angle encoding, FRQI, NEQR, hybrid).

- Error-mitigation strategy if any.

- Hardware-aware circuit synthesis, mapping, and transpilation level — controlled vocabulary {none reported, default transpilation (SDK default such as Qiskit transpile or Cirq optimizer), hardware-aware mapping (explicit qubit routing, native-gate decomposition, gate-count or depth optimisation against the executing topology), pulse-level optimisation (Qiskit Pulse, OpenPulse, optimal-control schedules)}. Quote-grounded; the as-reported value is one of the four levels.

- Circuit-complexity readout: qubit count, circuit depth, total gate count (or two-qubit gate count if separately reported), and shot count per circuit evaluation. Each of the four sub-fields is captured as the verbatim value if stated by the authors or 'not reported' otherwise. Each reported sub-field contributes one point to a quantum-resource-accounting completeness score (0–4) defined in §10.

- Extended image-encoding controlled vocabulary (supplementing the bullet above): basis encoding, angle encoding, amplitude encoding, FRQI, NEQR, NASS, MCRQI, NCQI, BRQI, hybrid, not applicable (e.g. for quantum annealing on tabular features); free text for novel encodings.

## 9.3 Imaging characterisation

- Modality (CT, PET, SPECT, MRI, ultrasound, X-ray, fundus, OCT, histopathology, dermoscopy, microscopy, other; multi-modal flag).

- Anatomic region or disease context (oncology, neurology, cardiology, ophthalmology, pulmonology, other).

- Imaging task (reconstruction, analysis, segmentation, classification, denoising, super-resolution, radiomics, fusion, instrumentation, dosimetry, other).

- Dataset (public benchmark with name; private clinical with size; synthetic with generator).

- Sample size (training, validation, test).

- Ionising-radiation flag (yes / no), derived deterministically from the modality field. Ionising = {PET, SPECT, CT, photon-counting CT, planar X-ray, mammography, fluoroscopy, gamma camera, scintigraphy, Compton camera, proton CT, proton radiography, EPID, in-vivo dosimetric imaging}; non-ionising = {MRI, ultrasound, OCT, fundus, histopathology, dermoscopy, microscopy}.

## 9.4 Validation and methodological quality

- Classical baseline present (yes / no), with identification of the baseline architecture or method.

- Reported performance metrics for both quantum method and classical baseline.

- Cross-validation strategy, external test set, statistical testing (yes / no, method).

- Reproducibility (code release, data release, pre-trained weights release).

- Reported computational cost (training time, inference time, quantum resource accounting).

- Baseline-rigour grade: none / weak (different data) / matched-data / matched-data + matched-compute / matched-data + matched-compute + statistical significance test. The grade is the highest level supported by quote-grounded evidence.

- Dataset-realism grade: toy (synthetic or downsampled MNIST-style images of edge length ≤32 pixels) / public benchmark (for example MedMNIST, BraTS, LIDC, OASIS) / private single-centre / private multi-centre / prospective clinical.

- Reproducibility tier (refinement of the bullet above): none / code released only / data released only / weights or circuit parameters released only / code + data + weights or circuit parameters.

- Reporting-standard adherence — checklist applied: none / CLAIM 2024 (Tejani et al., Radiology: AI, 2024) / TRIPOD+AI (Collins et al., BMJ 2024) / METRICS 2024 (Kocak et al., Insights Imaging 2024; radiomics only) / multiple. The score is 'none' if the paper does not reference any of these instruments.

## 9.5 Translational maturity

- Translational level (proof of concept, in-silico evaluation, retrospective clinical evaluation, prospective clinical evaluation).

- Clinical-impact claim made by the authors (verbatim or summarised).

- Independent clinical-impact assessment by the review team (low / moderate / high), with justification.

- Clinical-translation readiness composite score (0–4): sum of four binary criteria — (a) sample-size justification or power calculation reported by the authors, (b) external or independent validation set used, (c) regulatory pathway explicitly addressed (FDA 510(k), CE mark, MDR, or equivalent), (d) prospective clinical evaluation present. Each criterion is read from the manuscript and recorded as 0 or 1 based on quote-grounded evidence; no calculation or reproduction of the authors' results is performed by the review team.

# 10. Risk of bias and methodological-quality assessment

Standard clinical-trial risk-of-bias tools (Cochrane RoB 2, ROBINS-I) are not directly applicable to methodological papers on quantum-computing algorithms for medical imaging. A custom methodological-quality rubric will be used, with criteria locked before extraction:

- Dataset realism: toy / benchmark / real clinical.

- Sample-size justification reported by the authors (yes / no). The criterion is read from the manuscript; the review team does not perform an independent power calculation.

- Presence and identification of a classical baseline.

- Statistical testing with appropriate methods.

- External or independent validation.

- Honest reporting of quantum resources (qubit count, depth) and of hardware-vs-simulator distinction.

- Reproducibility (code and data availability).

- Quantum-resource accounting completeness (0–4): composite from the §9.2 circuit-complexity readout. Score = (qubit count reported) + (circuit depth reported) + (gate count reported) + (shot count reported). Each component contributes 0 or 1.

- Error-mitigation strategy clarity: none reported / readout-only / zero-noise extrapolation / probabilistic error cancellation / dynamical decoupling / T-REx / Clifford-data regression / composite. The reported method is quote-grounded; clarity = high if a named mitigation strategy is identifiable, low if only generic mention is present.

- Explainability mechanism reported: none / classical XAI (Grad-CAM, SHAP, LIME, attention, concept-bottleneck) / quantum XAI (QSHAP, QLRP, TSBA) / both. Tracked as a flag rather than a primary axis given the low expected prevalence in the corpus.

Each criterion will be rated low / moderate / high concern. A summary methodological-quality category (low / moderate / high concern) will be derived. Each rating will be performed by at least one AI pass and one human co-author cross-check; disagreements will be resolved by consensus discussion with a third co-author as adjudicator.

# 11. Synthesis methods

The synthesis will be narrative, supported by aggregate descriptive statistics and figures. No meta-analysis is planned because the heterogeneity of outcomes (different imaging tasks, modalities, datasets, metrics, baselines) precludes a meaningful pooled effect estimate.

Planned aggregate analyses:

- Distribution of papers by year, modality, imaging task, quantum paradigm, hardware platform.

- Distribution by country of corresponding author and by publication venue.

- Distribution by dataset type (real clinical, public benchmark, synthetic).

- Proportion of papers reporting a classical baseline; proportion reporting statistical testing; proportion releasing code or data.

- Translational-maturity distribution; methodological-quality distribution.

- Cross-tabulation of paradigm × imaging task and of modality × imaging task.

Sensitivity analyses will be performed by restricting the synthesis to papers that satisfy stricter criteria (real clinical dataset, classical baseline present, statistical testing performed). The robustness of headline statements to these restrictions will be reported.

# 12. Inter-rater reliability and audit

A stratified random sample of 120 records from the Stage 3 corpus has been prepared as a kappa-audit workbook. The workbook is balanced across the ten categories and the two confidence levels. A blinded human co-author will independently categorise these 120 records using the same taxonomy and decision rule. Agreement with the AI-assisted screening will be computed as Cohen's kappa overall and per-category. The acceptance threshold is overall kappa at least 0.61 (substantial agreement per Landis and Koch 1977). Discrepancies will be reviewed and reported.

For Stage 4 full-text screening, every AI verdict will be cross-checked by a human co-author against the full PDF. Disagreements will be resolved by consensus with a third co-author as adjudicator.

# 13. Reporting

The final review will be reported in conformance with the PRISMA 2020 reporting guideline. A PRISMA 2020 flow diagram will be included. A PRISMA 2020 checklist will be provided as supplementary material with item-by-item page references. The completed manuscript will be submitted to IEEE Transactions on Radiation and Plasma Medical Sciences in conformance with the journal's submission policies.

All scripts, prompt templates, model identifiers, audit logs, and verdict files generated during the screening pipeline will be deposited as supplementary materials in the OSF Project alongside the registration.

# 14. Use of generative AI

Generative AI is used in this review for record-level scope screening (Stage 3), full-text re-screening (Stage 4), data extraction (Stage 4 onwards), and subcategorisation (Stage 5). The model used is Claude Opus 4.7 with extended thinking, deployed via the Claude Code interactive environment under a Claude Pro Max subscription. Each task is executed by an isolated subagent context with a fixed prompt; outputs are validated against a JSON schema and require quote grounding against the source field. Every AI verdict that affects an inclusion or exclusion decision is cross-checked by a human co-author. All AI-assisted steps are logged with model identifier, prompt hash, raw response, and timestamp.

Generative AI is not used to write the substantive scientific argument, the discussion, or the interpretation of results in the manuscript. The use of generative AI in the screening and extraction pipeline will be disclosed explicitly in the Methods section of the manuscript and in the manuscript's acknowledgements, in line with IEEE and ICMJE guidance.

