# 16. Amendments policy

Substantive changes to the protocol after OSF deposit will be made via formal Registration Updates on OSF, each accompanied by a written justification. A Transparent Changes log will be maintained as a living document in the parent OSF Project, recording every deviation from the registered protocol with date, scope, and rationale. The log will be cited in the Methods section of the final manuscript.

Changes that do not require a Registration Update (because they do not affect the method, only the metadata): correction of typographic errors, addition of co-authors who joined after deposit, addition of supplementary files in the parent OSF Project, addition of the final manuscript DOI. These will still be reflected in the Transparent Changes log.

# 17. Known limitations

- Three databases (PubMed, Web of Science, IEEE Xplore); Scopus, Embase, Google Scholar were not searched.

- Five-year window (2021 to 2026); pre-2021 foundational papers are excluded by design and cited as background only.

- Single-keyword core concept ("quantum"); quantum-specific synonyms such as QML, VQE, QAOA, qubit, parameterised quantum circuit were not added to the strings.

- Retrospective registration with respect to Stages 1 to 3; prospective with respect to Stages 3.5 and onwards.

- AI-assisted screening introduces a model-specific bias that cannot be fully eliminated, only mitigated through quote-grounding, schema validation, audit logging, and human cross-check.

- Manual interventions in Stage 3 (single-record retries for one content-policy refusal; manual category retention for four PDFs without a parsable abstract header) are documented in the audit log but constitute a small residual departure from a fully automated pipeline.

- The Stage 3.5 sub-screen has a residual false-negative risk for QUANTUM_SENSING_BIOMED records that genuinely concern ionising-radiation imaging but evade keyword-based modality matching; this risk is estimated from a 10 percent random sample of stay_OUT verdicts and is reported in the manuscript.

- Two-pass design (v1 pilot then v2 full corpus). The v1 OA-only pilot was used to develop and lock the 10-category Stage-3 taxonomy. The v2 full-corpus pass applied that locked taxonomy to the expanded 6847-DOI corpus. The 2874 records identified by both passes were not re-screened; their v1 verdicts were reused with an audit flag. This design carries a residual risk that the v1-only piloting biased the taxonomy toward OA-visible subcategories; the manuscript reports this candidly in the Limitations section.

- v2 abstract-recovery title-loss bug (subsequently fixed). The v2 abstract recovery script initially discarded titles for records whose abstracts failed validation, affecting 868 records. A dedicated title-only retry recovered titles for 860 of them via Crossref; the remaining 8 had no metadata in any source and were PRISMA-excluded under "no metadata retrievable". This sequence is documented in v2_active/recovered_titles.jsonl and v2_active/no_metadata_retrievable.json in the supplementary file bundle.

